## Core data model
Design the domain so the system can eventually hold hundreds of SOPs without creating a custom form for every procedure.

An SOP should support:
- stable SOP ID
- title
- department / area / category
- status: draft / review / approved / retired
- version / revision
- responsible roles
- required competency
- frequency
- estimated duration
- purpose
- prerequisites
- PPE
- hazards
- equipment
- ingredients/materials where relevant
- pre-start checks
- ordered steps
- critical control information where relevant
- expected result
- common problems
- corrective action
- cleaning / shutdown where relevant
- escalation / emergency information
- manager verification requirement
- manufacturer documentation
- images
- animation/video media
- issue date
- review date
- approver
- audit history

## Flexible SOP step types
Do not hard-code SOPs into static pages.
Create reusable step/input types so an SOP step can request or display one or more of:
- instruction text
- acknowledgement / checkbox
- pass / flag / fail
- number
- temperature
- weight
- time
- timer
- date/time
- free note
- photo
- signature / sign-off
- QR scan
- NFC verification if supported later
- equipment meter reading
- label confirmation
- file/manual link
- image
- video / animation
- corrective-action trigger

Each submitted operational record must keep the original SOP version used at the time.

## Shift operations
Support reusable templates for:
- Opening
- Production
- Service
- Closing

Checklist/task states:
- Not started
- In progress
- Complete
- Flagged / corrective action required
- Not applicable (reason required when safety/compliance relevant)

Capture where appropriate:
- assigned person
- role
- scheduled time
- started_at
- completed_at
- actual duration
- readings
- notes
- photos
- corrective actions
- verification

## Temperature and food-safety recording
Support, without duplicating forms for each location:
- fridges
- freezers
- walk-ins
- delivery temperatures
- cooking checks
- hot holding
- cooling
- blast chilling
- reheating

Critical limits must be centrally configurable and versioned. Do not scatter magic numbers across UI code.

Out-of-range values must never be visually treated as a normal success.
Trigger the configured corrective-action workflow and record what was done.

Do not allow AI to invent or silently change food-safety limits.

## Cooling / leftovers workflow
Model a structured record including, as applicable:
- product / batch
- quantity
- service end time
- initial reading
- cooling method
- blast-chiller start
- blast-chiller finish
- final reading
- label created
- storage location
- planned reuse
- use-by decision
- staff member
- corrective action
- manager verification

## Storage / stock rotation
Support editable storage locations, including examples such as:
- Counter Fridge 1–3
- Upright Fridges
- Walk-in Fridge
- Upright Freezers
- Walk-in Freezer
- Dry Store 1–2

Support FIFO / FEFO workflows without assuming all products use identical date rules.

## Deliveries
A delivery workflow should be able to capture:
- supplier
- PO/reference if available
- time
- product / quantity
- packaging condition
- quality
- date coding
- temperature when required
- allergen/label information when relevant
- accepted / rejected / partial
- discrepancy
- photo
- person checking
- storage completion
- corrective action

## Equipment register
Every item should be capable of holding:
- equipment ID
- name
- manufacturer
- exact model
- serial number
- location
- photo
- rating-plate photo
- control-panel photo
- status
- manufacturer manual
- linked SOPs
- daily checks
- cleaning requirements
- maintenance requirements
- last service
- next service
- fault history
- training requirement
- QR identifier
- future NFC identifier

Never create model-specific operating instructions until the exact model/manual is confirmed.
Manufacturer instructions and competent-person approval take precedence over AI-generated draft text.

## QR behaviour
A QR attached to equipment should deep-link to the exact equipment record, not just a generic equipment category.
The quick equipment page should expose only what is useful at point of work, e.g.:
- Start-up
- Normal operation
- Cleaning
- Shutdown
- Safety
- Fault procedure
- Training
- Report fault

## Training
An SOP may include:
- text guide
- step images
- short animation
- short video
- full training video
- knowledge check
- practical assessment
- competency sign-off
- trainer
- retraining/review date

Viewing a video is not the same as practical competency.
Keep those states separate.

## Media / future AI-assisted capture
Design media relationships so a manager can later collect:
- equipment photos
- rating plates
- manuals
- narrated chef demonstrations
- training clips

AI may help convert collected material into draft SOP text, metadata or storyboards, but drafts must remain unapproved until reviewed by an authorised competent person.

## Kitchen production / covers
Kitchen operations may receive cover forecasts from the existing retreat/booking records and later connect to Parslia for recipe/menu intelligence.
Do not duplicate Parslia as a competing recipe master.

Support common cover planning ranges such as 20–200 plus custom cover counts, but never hard-code recipes or yields from guessed values.

## Task timing
Capture planned and actual duration for prep and cleaning tasks for planning and continuous improvement.
Never create speed leaderboards or incentives that encourage unsafe knife/equipment work.

## Offline behaviour
Essential Pocket functions should remain usable during temporary loss of internet:
- view cached approved critical SOPs
- view assigned shift/tasks already synchronised
- record permitted checklist/temperature/task data locally
- clearly show pending sync state
- sync safely when connection returns

Design for duplicate prevention / idempotency and conflict handling.
Do not queue sensitive payment-card data offline.

## Audit / integrity
For safety, compliance, SOP approval, competency and corrective actions, retain:
- actor
- timestamp
- original record/version
- change/command
- reason where relevant
- verification/approver

Use command endpoints for state-changing domain actions where consistent with the existing architecture.
Do not silently overwrite history.

## hotel operations integration direction
Vedanta owns its source records.
Future hotel/hospitality platform integration should happen through explicit adapters/API mappings, not by making an external platform the only database of record.

Keep external IDs in dedicated integration mapping fields/tables where appropriate.
Do not contaminate core domain keys with provider-specific assumptions.

## Design language
Extend `docs/design.md`; do not replace it with a generic SaaS theme.
Target: calm, premium hospitality, professional kitchen usability.

Existing visual language to respect:
- stone/paper surfaces
- green-black ink
- restrained lake/moss/marigold accents
- brick/red only for real danger/cancellation
- Newsreader for display headings
- IBM Plex Sans for operational UI

For kitchen mobile use:
- large touch targets
- excellent contrast
- simple hierarchy
- minimal typing
- one-hand operation where practical
- no decorative animation in operational screens
- animation/video only when instructional

Red should communicate a real exception, safety failure, destructive action or urgent issue.

## Safety / compliance rules
- Never let AI invent a safety procedure and publish it automatically.
- Never publish equipment instructions without exact equipment identification and approval.
- Never hide an out-of-range reading.
- Never let a user mark a safety-critical failure complete without the configured corrective action/verification.
- Do not weaken allergen/privacy/security controls already in the repository.
- No PAN/CVV storage.

## Implementation order
Do not build every idea at once.

Phase 1 foundation:
1. inspect current SOP/manual/task/compliance patterns
2. define additive database/domain/API contracts
3. SOP library + SOP viewer
4. Today / My Shift kitchen view
5. opening and closing templates
6. temperature recording with corrective action
7. cleaning task templates
8. equipment register and QR deep-link structure
9. PWA install/offline foundation for Pocket
10. manager SOP/equipment authoring basics
11. audit trail + permissions
12. tests + docs

Later phases:
- delivery receiving
- cooling/blast chilling/leftovers
- storage/FEFO
- training and competency
- rich media/animations
- maintenance integration
- production/cover planning
- hotel-platform adapters
- native wrapper only if justified

## Definition of done
A module is not done because a page renders.
Done means, as applicable:
- domain model
- permissions
- additive migration
- API contract
- validation
- manager UI
- Pocket/mobile UI
- error states
- offline behaviour where required
- audit/event history
- tests
- accessibility/usability
- documentation
- regression check against existing Vedanta OS

When uncertain, preserve existing behaviour and document the conflict instead of guessing.